#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Decide which apple-ci legs a run needs, and check afterwards that each did what was planned.

spec §21.6. A pull request runs `apple-platform` only when it changes something an Apple build can
read, and never runs the two conformance jobs, `apple-conformance-core` and
`apple-conformance-ipad-iphone`; a push to `main` reuses same-tree PR proof only when both exact-head
runs are successful in their latest attempts. Otherwise it and a plain dispatch run every leg.
--conformance-only true runs conformance and Discover; --discover-only true runs only the three
Discover jobs as a diagnostic, without qualifying as a full release run. The
required `apple-ci` job always runs, because a check that never reports blocks a merge forever.

  plan-apple-legs plan --event EVENT [--changed-files FILE] [--conformance-only true|false] [--discover-only true|false]
      Prints `platform=true|false`, `conformance=true|false` and `discover=true|false` as step outputs on stdout, and the
      reason on stderr. On a pull request the changed paths come from FILE (one per line) or, when
      it is omitted, from `git diff --name-only HEAD^1 HEAD` on the merge commit Actions checks out.
      Unknown events plan every leg. An unreadable or empty PR diff plans the platform leg.
      --conformance-only defaults to false and applies only to workflow_dispatch; it cannot
      suppress the platform leg on push or change a pull request's plan.

  plan-apple-legs require-leg PLANNED RESULT
      Exits 0 only for (PLANNED=true, RESULT=success) or (PLANNED=false, RESULT=skipped). Every
      other pair fails, including an empty PLANNED, which is what a plan job that never produced
      its outputs leaves behind.

What counts as Apple-affecting is an EXCLUSION list: a path is Apple-affecting unless it matches
NOT_APPLE below, so a new top-level directory or a new tool runs the Apple legs until someone
decides otherwise. `tools/test-plan-apple-legs` proves that no excluded tool is named by anything
the Apple legs read.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fnmatch
import http.client
import json
import os
from pathlib import Path
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import urllib.request


